#h1{Where literate programming came from, and where this is going}

Literate programming began as a way of writing a program to be read. In Knuth's account
#cite{knuth1984lp} a program is a work of exposition: a web of parts, presented in the order
that is best for a person, from which the order the machine needs is derived. As far as the
author of this document knows, such programs were first worked out on paper, as a text for
people, and only then entered into a computer, and Knuth's own programs are the model of the
kind. The paper was the point; the computer was how the program got to run.

The tools that followed were, for the most part, made by and for people who write papers and
books. They put the document first and treat a program as an illustration in it, and they are a
poor fit for a team that maintains software which already exists, in a language and a repository
it did not choose. Ramsey's simplification #cite{ramsey1994lp} removed the dependence on a
language, and that is the line this toolkit follows. What it adds is for the working programmer:
tools for the day somebody edits the generated code, for programs that are being converted and
not written, and for gaps that an assistant can fill.

Notebooks are what most people meet today, and a notebook is not a literate program in the sense
above. Its cells run in the order in which they are arranged, so the order of the explanation
and the order of execution are one and the same, which is the constraint that literate
programming exists to remove. There are no named parts that are defined in one place and used in
another, nothing is tangled into the files of a program, and hidden state lets a notebook read
well and still fail when it is run from the top. Notebooks are good for exploring data. They are
a different thing, and this toolkit does not try to be one.

Two things follow for the shape of this document. The paper is a first-class output: weave
writes LaTeX, so the program can be printed and read away from a screen, which is how a book
should be read, and the document is written to be read from the first page. And the order of the
chapters is the order of importance. The ideas come first, and then the tools, with the
gap-filling assistant near the end of the story. What a reader could write for themselves, the
plain details of the program, is at the bottom, in the appendix: the entry point, the argument
parser, a text comparison, the installer, the license of the parser, the files of the skill, the
landing page and the small helper functions that are named by their use.
